window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u":"index.html","p":"Professional Practices and Documentation","s":"","t":"System Administration and Maintenance · Albao Group This site is both our report and a live example of it. Every page follows the documentation practices it describes, so the site itself is the demonstration. Start reading → See sample documents Try the search"},{"u":"index.html#report-topics","p":"Professional Practices and Documentation","s":"Report topics","t":"The report is divided into three topics. Each one is a separate page with its own table of contents, metadata and revision details. 1 Documentation Standards What good sysadmin documentation looks like: types, qualities, style rules, naming, versioning and document control. Read topic → 2 IT Policies & Compliance Policy vs. standard vs. procedure, the core IT policies, the policy lifecycle, laws such as the Data Privacy Act, and audits. Read topic → 3 Ethics in System Administration Privacy, privileged access, honesty and conflicts of interest, with codes of ethics, a decision framework and interactive scenarios. Read topic →"},{"u":"index.html#why-documentation","p":"Professional Practices and Documentation","s":"Why documentation matters","t":"System administrators keep services running, often under pressure and often at 3 a.m. Documentation turns one person's knowledge into the team's knowledge. Without it, every outage starts from zero and every staff departure takes knowledge with it. Without documentation “Only one person knows how the backup works.” Outages last longer because steps are guessed. Audits fail for lack of evidence. New hires take months to become useful. With documentation Anyone on shift can follow the runbook. Changes are repeatable and reversible. Policies and logs prove compliance. Onboarding is self-service."},{"u":"index.html#features","p":"Professional Practices and Documentation","s":"Documentation features shown on this site","t":"A documentation system is more than a pile of text files. These are the features a good documentation website offers. Each one is used somewhere on this site, so you can try them out. Navigation & structure Sidebar, breadcrumbs and previous/next links. See every page. Full-text search Press Ctrl K or / and search for “password”. Table of contents “On this page” panel that highlights the section you are reading. Document control Document ID, version, owner, status and review date. See the SOP . Status badges Draft, In Review and Approved, shown at the top of each page. Revision history A change log at the end of every sample document. Callouts Note, Tip, Warning and Danger boxes for important information. Code blocks Commands with a one-click Copy button. Tabs The same steps for Linux and Windows. Try it . Tables & diagrams Inventories, IP plans and a network diagram . Checklists Interactive, with progress saved in your browser. Try it . Templates Copy or download ready-made Markdown templates. Templates . Glossary Searchable list of terms, plus hover definitions on SOP -style abbreviations. Deep links Hover a heading and click # to link straight to that section. Print / PDF Clean print layout. Use the printer icon under any page title. Dark mode The sun/moon icon in the top bar. Your choice is remembered. Feedback “Was this page helpful?” at the end of every page. Docs as code Plain files in Git, so every change is reviewed and tracked. How it works ."},{"u":"index.html#site-map","p":"Professional Practices and Documentation","s":"How this site is organized","t":"Good documentation has a predictable structure, so readers know where to look before they search. This site uses the same layout as most professional docs portals: Section Purpose Pages Report Topics Explanations: the why and what Standards , Policies & Compliance , Ethics Sample Documents Real-world examples a sysadmin team writes SOP , Runbook , Incident Report , Change Request , Network & Inventory Resources Reference material to reuse Templates , Glossary , About & References Presenter tip For a quick walkthrough: open a topic page, press Ctrl K and search for backup , open the SOP to show document control and tabs, then switch to dark mode and print a page to PDF."},{"u":"pages/about.html","p":"About & References","s":"","t":"Resources Who made this site, how it is built, how it changed, and where the information comes from."},{"u":"pages/about.html#about-report","p":"About & References","s":"About this report","t":"Course System Administration and Maintenance Report Professional Practices and Documentation Topics Documentation standards · IT policies and compliance · Ethical issues in system administration Prepared by Albao Group"},{"u":"pages/about.html#members","p":"About & References","s":"Group members","t":"Name Part of the report [Member name] Documentation Standards [Member name] IT Policies and Compliance [Member name] Ethical Issues in System Administration [Member name] Demonstration (this website)"},{"u":"pages/about.html#how-this-site-works","p":"About & References","s":"How this site works","t":"The site is itself an example of docs as code : Plain files: every page is an HTML file. There is no database and no server-side code, so it runs offline straight from a folder. Version control: all files live in a Git repository on GitHub . Every change is recorded with its author, date and reason. Single source of truth: the navigation menu is defined once in assets/js/site.js and used by every page. Generated search index: a small script reads every page and builds the search index automatically. Free hosting: the site can be published with GitHub Pages. Every change pushed to the repository goes live automatically. Simple-Documentation-Demo/ ├── index.html ← home page ├── pages/ │ ├── documentation-standards.html │ ├── it-policies-compliance.html │ ├── ethics-in-sysadmin.html │ ├── templates.html │ ├── glossary.html │ ├── about.html │ └── samples/ ← sample documents ├── assets/ │ ├── css/style.css ← design, dark mode, print layout │ └── js/ │ ├── site.js ← navigation, search, interactive features │ └── search-index.js ← generated, do not edit by hand └── scripts/build-search-index.mjs ← regenerates the search index"},{"u":"pages/about.html#adding-a-page","p":"About & References","s":"Adding or updating a page","t":"Copy an existing page in the same folder and change its title, data-page and content. Add it to the navigation in the NAV list at the top of assets/js/site.js . Rebuild the search index. node scripts/build-search-index.mjs Update the page's data-updated date and version , then commit with a clear message, e.g. docs: add VPN setup SOP ."},{"u":"pages/about.html#changelog","p":"About & References","s":"Site revision history","t":"Version Date Change 1.0 2026-10-28 First release: three report topics, five sample documents, templates, glossary, search, dark mode and print layout."},{"u":"pages/about.html#references","p":"About & References","s":"References","t":""},{"u":"pages/about.html#ref-standards","p":"About & References","s":"Standards and frameworks","t":"International Organization for Standardization. (2015). ISO 9001:2015 Quality management systems: Requirements (clause 7.5, Documented information). ISO. International Organization for Standardization & International Electrotechnical Commission. (2022). ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Information security, cybersecurity and privacy protection: Information security management systems: Requirements . ISO. iso.org/standard/27001 ISO/IEC/IEEE. (2022). ISO/IEC/IEEE 26514:2022 Systems and software engineering: Design and development of information for users . ISO/IEC/IEEE. (2019). ISO/IEC/IEEE 15289:2019 Systems and software engineering: Content of life-cycle information items (documentation) . National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2024). The NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0 . nist.gov/cyberframework National Institute of Standards and Technology. SP 800-63B Digital Identity Guidelines: Authentication and Lifecycle Management and SP 800-88 Guidelines for Media Sanitization . Center for Internet Security. CIS Critical Security Controls v8.1 . cisecurity.org/controls PCI Security Standards Council. PCI Data Security Standard v4.x . pcisecuritystandards.org Procida, D. Diátaxis: A systematic approach to technical documentation authoring . diataxis.fr Google. Google developer documentation style guide . developers.google.com/style Eastlake, D., & Panitz, A. (1999). RFC 2606: Reserved Top Level DNS Names . IETF. rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2606 Arkko, J., Cotton, M., & Vegoda, L. (2010). RFC 5737: IPv4 Address Blocks Reserved for Documentation . IETF. rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5737"},{"u":"pages/about.html#ref-laws","p":"About & References","s":"Laws and regulations","t":"Republic of the Philippines. (2012). Republic Act No. 10173: Data Privacy Act of 2012 . National Privacy Commission. privacy.gov.ph/data-privacy-act Republic of the Philippines. (2012). Republic Act No. 10175: Cybercrime Prevention Act of 2012 . Official Gazette. officialgazette.gov.ph Republic of the Philippines. (2000). Republic Act No. 8792: Electronic Commerce Act of 2000 . European Union. (2016). Regulation (EU) 2016/679: General Data Protection Regulation . eur-lex.europa.eu"},{"u":"pages/about.html#ref-ethics","p":"About & References","s":"Ethics","t":"Association for Computing Machinery. (2018). ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct . acm.org/code-of-ethics USENIX / SAGE and LOPSA. The System Administrators' Code of Ethics . ISC2. ISC2 Code of Ethics . isc2.org/ethics"},{"u":"pages/about.html#ref-books","p":"About & References","s":"Books","t":"Limoncelli, T. A., Hogan, C. J., & Chalup, S. R. (2016). The Practice of System and Network Administration (3rd ed.). Addison-Wesley."},{"u":"pages/about.html#disclaimer","p":"About & References","s":"Disclaimer","t":"This site was made for educational purposes. Example Corp and all names, systems, addresses and records in the sample documents are fictional. Summaries of laws and standards are simplified for teaching and are not legal advice. Always consult the official text."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"","t":"Topic 1 of 3 Documentation standards are the agreed rules for what we document, how we write it, and how we keep it accurate . They make documents from different people look, read and behave the same way."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#what-is-documentation","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"What is system documentation?","t":"System documentation is the written record of how an organization's IT environment is built, configured, operated and supported. It covers the hardware and software you run, how they connect, the procedures for working on them, and the history of what has changed. A useful test is the “hit by a bus” test (sometimes called the lottery test ): if the only person who knows a system suddenly left, could someone else keep it running using only the documentation? If not, the documentation is incomplete. Note Documentation is written for a specific reader. A runbook is for the on-call admin at 3 a.m. A policy is for every employee. A network diagram is for engineers and auditors. Decide who will read it before you start writing."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#types","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Types of system administration documentation","t":"A typical IT team keeps several kinds of documents. Each has a different purpose and audience. Type What it contains Example on this site Policy High-level rules and the reasons for them (“what” and “why”) Core IT policies SOP Step-by-step instructions for a routine task New User Account SOP Runbook / Playbook Operational steps for running a service and responding to alerts Backup & Restore Runbook Network diagram How devices, networks and VLAN s connect Network diagram Asset inventory Hardware, software, licenses, owners and warranty dates Server inventory Configuration record How a system is set up (its baseline) so it can be rebuilt IP address plan Change record What changed, why, who approved it and how to undo it Change Request Incident report What went wrong, the impact, the root cause and the fix Incident Report Knowledge base (KB) article Short answers to common problems for users or the help desk KB template Disaster recovery (DR) plan How to restore services after a major failure Full restore steps"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#qualities","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Qualities of good documentation","t":"Whatever its type, good documentation has the same qualities. A simple way to remember them is the seven Cs : Quality Meaning Ask yourself Correct Technically accurate and tested Did I actually run these steps? Clear Plain language, one meaning per sentence Could a new hire follow this? Concise No filler. Every sentence has a job. Can I remove this sentence? Complete Covers prerequisites, steps, verification and rollback What happens if a step fails? Consistent Same terms, format and structure across all documents Does it match our template? Current Updated when the system changes, with a version and date When was this last reviewed? Controlled Has an owner, an approval status and a change history Who is responsible for this page?"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#recognized-standards","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Recognized standards and frameworks","t":"Organizations don't have to invent documentation rules from scratch. These published standards and frameworks are widely used as a reference: Standard / framework What it says about documentation ISO/IEC/IEEE 26514 How to design and develop information for users (user guides, help, online documentation). ISO/IEC/IEEE 15289 What content each life-cycle document (plans, procedures, reports, records) should include. ISO 9001 (clause 7.5) “Documented information” must be identified, reviewed, approved, version-controlled, protected and kept available. This is the basis of document control . ISO/IEC 27001 (Annex A 5.37) Operating procedures for information processing facilities must be documented and made available to the people who need them. ITIL Its knowledge management , change enablement and incident management practices all depend on maintained records. Diátaxis A popular framework that splits documentation into four kinds: tutorials , how-to guides , reference and explanation . Tip This site follows the Diátaxis split. The Report Topics are explanation , the SOP and runbook are how-to guides , and the inventory, glossary and templates are reference ."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#style-guide","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Writing style rules","t":"A style guide makes documents written by different people read as if one person wrote them. These are common rules used by IT teams."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#style-procedures","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Writing procedures","t":"Number the steps and put one action in each step . Start each step with a verb in the imperative mood: “Restart the service”, not “The service should be restarted”. Show the expected result after important steps so the reader knows it worked. List prerequisites (access, tools, approvals) before step 1. Always include verification and rollback steps. Put commands in code blocks without the shell prompt ( $ or # ) so they can be copied and pasted safely."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#style-language","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Language and formatting","t":"Use active voice and plain English. Short sentences are better. Define acronyms the first time they appear, e.g. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP). Write dates in ISO 8601 format, YYYY-MM-DD (e.g. 2026-10-28 ), which no reader can misread. Use clear placeholders for values the reader replaces, e.g. <username> , and explain them. In public examples use reserved names and addresses, such as example.com (RFC 2606) and 192.0.2.0/24 (RFC 5737), so you never expose or target a real system. Give every screenshot or diagram a caption and alt text , and never rely on an image alone for a critical step."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#style-examples","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Example: a poor step vs. a good step","t":"Poor “Then you should probably restart the web server and check if it's working again.” Vague (“probably”, “check”) No command and no expected result Good Restart the web server: sudo systemctl restart nginx Confirm it is running: systemctl status nginx Expected: Active: active (running)"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#naming","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Naming conventions","t":"Consistent names make documents and systems easy to find, sort and recognize. Agree on a convention and write it down. The convention itself is documentation. Item Convention Example Document ID TYPE-AREA-NNN SOP-ACC-001 , POL-SEC-002 File name ID_Short-Title_vX.Y.ext SOP-ACC-001_New-User-Provisioning_v1.2.md Incident / change ID TYPE-YEAR-NNNN INC-2026-0142 , CHG-2026-0088 Hostname role-number (optionally with a site prefix) web-01 , mnl-fs-01 Dates ISO 8601 2026-10-28"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#document-control","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Document control and versioning","t":"Document control is the process that makes sure people always use the current, approved version of a document. Every controlled document starts with a metadata block like this one: Document control (example) Approved Document ID SOP-ACC-001 Version 1.2 Owner IT Operations Approved by IT Manager Effective date 2026-09-01 Next review 2027-09-01 Classification Internal Related POL-SEC-002"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#version-numbers","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Version numbers","t":"0.1 – 0.9 : drafts, not yet approved for use. 1.0 : the first approved, published version. 1.1, 1.2 … : minor changes such as corrections or clarifications. 2.0 : a major change, such as a new process, which needs re-approval."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#statuses","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Document status","t":"Draft In Review Approved Superseded The status tells readers whether they can rely on a document. Only Approved documents should be followed in production. Superseded versions are archived, not deleted, because auditors may need them."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#lifecycle","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"The documentation lifecycle","t":"Figure 1. A document moves from planning to archive. Maintenance loops back to writing."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#review-cycle","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"When to review a document","t":"On a fixed schedule, at least once a year (the “Next review” date). Whenever the system it describes changes . Updating the docs should be part of every change request. After an incident shows that the documentation was wrong or missing. When the document owner leaves or changes role. Assign a new owner immediately."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#docs-structure","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Organizing a documentation repository","t":"Store documentation in one agreed location , not scattered across email, chat and personal notes. A simple, predictable folder structure helps: it-docs/ ├── README.md ← start here: what is where, who owns it ├── policies/ │ ├── POL-SEC-001_Acceptable-Use-Policy.md │ └── POL-SEC-002_Password-Policy.md ├── procedures/ ← SOPs │ └── SOP-ACC-001_New-User-Provisioning.md ├── runbooks/ │ └── RB-BKP-002_Nightly-Backup-and-Restore.md ├── network/ │ ├── NET-001_Network-Diagram.drawio │ └── NET-002_IP-Address-Plan.md ├── inventory/ │ └── assets.csv ├── incidents/2026/ │ └── INC-2026-0142_File-Server-Outage.md ├── changes/2026/ │ └── CHG-2026-0088_Web-Server-Patching.md └── templates/"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#docs-as-code","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Docs as code","t":"Many teams now treat documentation like source code. They write it in Markdown , store it in Git , review changes through pull requests , and publish it automatically as a website. This gives you version history, peer review and accountability for free. This site is built that way. See how this site works ."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#tools","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Tools for documentation","t":"Category Examples Best for Wiki / knowledge base Confluence, BookStack, Wiki.js, DokuWiki, SharePoint Easy team editing and searching Docs as code Markdown + Git with MkDocs, Docusaurus, Sphinx or Hugo Versioned, peer-reviewed documentation Diagrams draw.io (diagrams.net), Microsoft Visio, Mermaid Network and architecture diagrams Inventory / CMDB NetBox, Snipe-IT, GLPI Assets, IP addresses, racks, licenses Ticketing (ITSM) Jira Service Management, ServiceNow, osTicket Incident and change records Secrets management Bitwarden, KeePassXC, HashiCorp Vault Passwords and keys, which never go in documents"},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#security","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Documentation and security","t":"Documentation describes how to access and control your systems, so it is sensitive in its own right. Never put secrets in documentation Passwords, private keys, API tokens and recovery codes belong in a password manager or secrets vault . The document should say where the credential is stored (“see vault entry fs-01 / root ”), never what it is. Label every document with a classification (Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). Restrict who can edit controlled documents, and keep an edit history. Keep an offline copy of critical runbooks. If the wiki server is down, you still need the recovery steps."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#common-mistakes","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Common mistakes","t":"“Doc rot” : written once and never updated, so it is now wrong. Assumed knowledge : “just fix the config on the usual box”. No owner , so nobody feels responsible for keeping it accurate. Walls of text with no headings, steps or tables. Screenshots only , which can't be searched or copied and go out of date quickly. Missing dates and versions , so readers can't tell if it is still valid."},{"u":"pages/documentation-standards.html#summary","p":"Documentation Standards","s":"Key takeaways","t":"Can you explain these to the class? The main types of sysadmin documentation and who reads each one The seven Cs of good documentation At least two published standards (e.g. ISO 9001 clause 7.5, ISO/IEC 27001) Style rules for writing procedures What a document control block contains, and how version numbers work Why secrets never go into documentation"},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"","t":"Topic 3 of 3 Administrators hold the “keys to the kingdom”. With root or domain admin rights you can read anyone's email, open any file, view browsing history and even edit the logs that record what you did. Ethics is about how you use that power when nobody is watching."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#why-ethics","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"Why ethics matters in system administration","t":"Most people can only do what their account allows. System administrators set the permissions, so technical controls alone cannot stop a dishonest admin. That is why organizations depend on the trust and professional integrity of their IT staff. Legal What the law requires or forbids, e.g. the Data Privacy Act and Cybercrime Prevention Act. Breaking it can lead to fines or prison. Ethical What is right , even when no law or policy covers the situation. Something can be legal and still be unethical. Note “Can I do this?” is a technical question. “ Should I do this?” is an ethical one. Sysadmins have to ask both."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#key-issues","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"Key ethical issues","t":""},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#privacy","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"1. Privacy and user monitoring","t":"Admins can technically see users' emails, files, chat messages and web history. Having the ability does not give you the right to use it. Ethical practice Access personal data only when your duties require it . Monitor only what the AUP discloses and the law allows. Collect the minimum data needed (proportionality). Get written authorization before investigating a user. Unethical practice Reading a coworker's email out of curiosity. Looking up a celebrity's or ex-partner's records. Secretly installing monitoring software. Sharing what you saw in users' files."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#privileged-access","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"2. Abuse of privileged access","t":"Using admin rights for personal benefit or beyond your authorization: granting yourself access to HR or payroll data, bypassing controls “because it's faster”, or deleting log entries. Under the Cybercrime Prevention Act, accessing a system without right can be a crime even for an employee. Safeguards: least privilege, separate admin accounts, privileged access management (PAM), tamper-resistant logs sent to a separate server, and a “two-person rule” for the most sensitive actions."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#confidentiality","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"3. Confidentiality","t":"Admins regularly see confidential information: salaries, medical records, legal cases, business plans. It must not be disclosed, discussed or used for personal gain, even after you leave the organization. Many employers ask IT staff to sign a non-disclosure agreement (NDA) ."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#honesty","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"4. Honesty and transparency","t":"Everyone makes mistakes: a wrong command, a missed backup, a misconfigured firewall. The ethical response is to admit it quickly , fix it, and document it so the team can learn. Hiding mistakes, falsifying reports or concealing a data breach makes the damage worse. Under the Data Privacy Act, concealing a breach is a crime. Blameless culture Good teams write blameless incident reports that focus on what failed and how to prevent it , not who to punish. This makes people more willing to report honestly. See the sample incident report ."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#licensing","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"5. Software licensing and intellectual property","t":"Installing unlicensed or pirated software, using one license on more machines than allowed, or ignoring open-source license terms is unethical and illegal, and the organization is liable. Admins should keep a software license inventory and refuse requests to install unlicensed software."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#conflicts","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"6. Conflicts of interest","t":"A conflict of interest exists when personal interests could influence professional decisions. Examples include accepting gifts from vendors, recommending a friend's company, or choosing a product because you get a commission. Disclose conflicts, follow the gift policy, and make purchasing decisions on documented criteria ."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#pressure","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"7. Pressure from management","t":"Sometimes the unethical request comes from above: “Delete those logs before the auditors arrive.” “Give me access to my team's private messages.” “Don't tell customers about the breach.” Following orders does not remove your responsibility. Ask for the request in writing and check it against policy and law. Escalate through proper channels: your manager's manager, HR, Legal, Compliance or the DPO. Use the organization's whistleblowing procedure if internal channels fail."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#competence","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"8. Professional competence and due care","t":"It is unethical to accept work you are not qualified to do without saying so, to skip testing, or to make risky changes without approval. Admins have a duty to keep their skills current, follow change management, and be honest about what they don't know."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#ai-tools","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"9. Emerging issues: AI tools and workplace surveillance","t":"AI assistants: pasting logs, configuration files or customer data into a public AI chatbot can disclose personal data and secrets to a third party. Use only approved tools, and remove sensitive data first. Employee monitoring software (screen capture, keystroke logging, productivity scoring) raises serious privacy concerns. It must be lawful, proportionate, disclosed and approved, and should not be secretly configured by IT. Automation: scripts and bots act with the admin's authority, so their actions are still the admin's responsibility."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#social","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"10. Social and environmental responsibility","t":"Secure disposal: wipe or destroy drives before disposal or donation so that old data doesn't leak. E-waste: recycle equipment through accredited handlers instead of dumping it. Accessibility: make systems and documentation usable by people with disabilities."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#codes-of-ethics","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"Professional codes of ethics","t":"Professional organizations publish codes of ethics to guide practitioners. They are not laws, but employers and peers expect professionals to follow them."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#sage-code","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"The System Administrators' Code of Ethics","t":"Originally written by SAGE (USENIX's system administrators' group) and later adopted by LOPSA (the League of Professional System Administrators), this code has ten principles: Principle In short Professionalism Treat everyone fairly. Don't let personal feelings affect how you treat people. Personal integrity Be honest about your skills and your mistakes. Avoid or disclose conflicts of interest. Privacy Access private information only when your duties require it, and protect its confidentiality. Laws and policies Learn the laws and policies that apply to your work, and help others learn them. Communication Keep management, users and colleagues informed. Listen to their needs. System integrity Keep systems reliable, available and fit for their purpose. Education Keep improving your skills and share your knowledge. Responsibility to the computing community Cooperate with others to keep networks and computing resources healthy. Social responsibility Support sound policies and laws that reflect these principles. Ethical responsibility Build a safe, healthy workplace. Report risks, give and accept honest criticism, and credit others' work."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#acm-code","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct","t":"The Association for Computing Machinery's code (updated 2018) applies to all computing professionals. Its general principles include: contribute to society and human well-being, avoid harm , be honest and trustworthy, be fair and don't discriminate, respect the work required to produce new ideas, respect privacy , and honor confidentiality ."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#isc2-code","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"ISC2 Code of Ethics","t":"For security professionals (e.g. CISSP holders), it has four canons: protect society and the infrastructure; act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly and legally; provide diligent and competent service; and advance and protect the profession."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#decision-framework","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"A framework for ethical decisions","t":"When you face a difficult situation, work through it step by step instead of acting on impulse: Identify the issue What exactly is being asked or is happening? Whose interests are involved? Check policy and law What do the AUP, other company policies, the Data Privacy Act and the Cybercrime Act say? Identify the stakeholders Users, the organization, customers, colleagues, the public, and you. Consider the options List at least three possible actions and the likely consequences of each. Consult Talk to your supervisor, HR, Legal, the DPO or a trusted senior colleague. Use the codes of ethics. Act and document Choose the most defensible option, then write down what you decided, why, and who approved it. Reflect Afterwards, consider whether a policy or procedure should change to prevent the dilemma next time."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#quick-tests","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"Quick tests","t":"Test Ask yourself Front-page test Would I be comfortable if this appeared in the news with my name on it? Audit-log test Would I be comfortable if my manager reviewed the log of exactly what I did? Policy test Is this clearly allowed by a written, approved policy? Reversal test How would I feel if someone did this to my data or account?"},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#scenarios","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"Scenarios: what would you do?","t":"Use these with the class. Choose an answer to see the discussion. Scenario 1 · Privacy Your manager asks you to quietly open an employee's mailbox because they suspect the employee is job hunting. There is no written request. A. Do it. Your manager is your boss. B. Politely decline until there is a written request approved through HR/Legal under the policy, and make a note of the request. C. Warn the employee that their manager is checking on them. Mailbox access must have a legitimate purpose and be authorized under the AUP and the law. Job hunting is not a valid reason, and accessing the mailbox without authorization could violate the Data Privacy Act. A written, approved request protects everyone, including you. Warning the employee would breach confidentiality. Scenario 2 · Policy violation While cleaning up disk space on the file server, you notice a coworker's folder full of pirated movies. A. Ignore it. It's not your business. B. Delete the files yourself and say nothing. C. Stop browsing, note the location (without opening or copying the files), and report it through the channel the AUP specifies. You found it while doing legitimate work, and it violates the AUP and copyright law, which creates risk for the organization. Report it through the proper channel and let the authorized people decide. Deleting the files yourself could destroy evidence, and investigating further is outside your role. Scenario 3 · Honesty You accidentally deleted a shared department folder. Last night's backup exists and nobody has noticed yet. A. Restore it quietly. No harm done. B. Restore it, tell your supervisor and the affected users, and write a short incident report. C. Restore it and blame a sync error if anyone asks. The backup is from last night , so any work saved today is lost. Users need to know so they can redo it. Being honest about mistakes is a core principle of the sysadmin code of ethics. A blameless incident report also helps prevent it happening again, for example by adding a confirmation step to the procedure. Scenario 4 · Conflict of interest A backup software vendor offers you a free smartphone if you recommend their product to management. A. Accept. It won't change your opinion. B. Decline (or disclose it under the gift policy) and evaluate all products against documented criteria. C. Accept and recommend them. Everyone does it. Even if your judgment isn't affected, accepting creates a conflict of interest and damages trust. Follow the gift and conflict-of-interest policy, and base your recommendation on a documented comparison (cost, features, support, security) that others can review. Scenario 5 · AI tools You are troubleshooting an error and want to paste a server log into a public AI chatbot. The log contains customer names, email addresses and an API token. A. Paste the whole log. It's the fastest way. B. Paste it, but delete the chat history afterwards. C. Remove or mask the personal data and secrets first, and use only an AI tool your organization has approved. Sending personal data to an unapproved third party is a disclosure under the Data Privacy Act, and a leaked API token is a security incident. Deleting the chat afterwards doesn't undo the disclosure. Mask the data, revoke the token if it was exposed, and follow your organization's AI use policy."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#documentation-ethics","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"How documentation supports ethical practice","t":"This topic links back to the other two. Documentation makes ethical behaviour easier and provable: Written policies make expectations clear, so admins know what they may and may not do. Written authorizations (e.g. for accessing a mailbox) protect the admin and the user. Logs and change records provide accountability and deter abuse. Honest incident reports turn mistakes into improvements. Documented decision criteria prevent conflicts of interest from influencing purchases."},{"u":"pages/ethics-in-sysadmin.html#summary","p":"Ethical Issues in System Administration","s":"Key takeaways","t":"Admin access means power, and power requires trust and integrity. The main issues are privacy, privileged access, confidentiality, honesty, licensing, conflicts of interest and pressure from management. Follow professional codes such as the System Administrators' Code of Ethics and the ACM Code. Use a structured framework for hard decisions, and document what you decided and why."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html","p":"Glossary","s":"","t":"Resources Definitions of the terms used on this site. A shared vocabulary keeps documentation consistent: everyone means the same thing by “incident”, “standard” or “runbook”. div\" data-filter-group=\".glossary-group\" data-filter-count=\"glossary-count\"> A B C D E G I K L M N P R S V A"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-aup","p":"Glossary","s":"Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)","t":"A policy that defines what users may and may not do with an organization's computers, network and internet access."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-access-review","p":"Glossary","s":"Access review","t":"A periodic check that every user's access rights are still needed and approved. Unneeded access is removed."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-audit","p":"Glossary","s":"Audit","t":"An independent examination of whether controls, policies and procedures are designed properly and actually followed."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-audit-trail","p":"Glossary","s":"Audit trail","t":"A chronological record (logs, change records, approvals) showing who did what and when. B"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-backout-plan","p":"Glossary","s":"Backout plan","t":"The documented steps to undo a change and return a system to its previous state if the change fails. Also called a rollback plan."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-baseline","p":"Glossary","s":"Baseline","t":"The approved, documented configuration of a system, used as a reference for building, auditing and restoring it."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-byod","p":"Glossary","s":"Bring Your Own Device (BYOD)","t":"Allowing employees to use personal devices for work, under rules set in a BYOD policy. C"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-cab","p":"Glossary","s":"Change Advisory Board (CAB)","t":"A group that reviews and approves significant changes to IT systems."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-change-management","p":"Glossary","s":"Change management","t":"The process for requesting, assessing, approving, implementing and reviewing changes so they don't cause unplanned outages. ITIL calls it change enablement ."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-cia","p":"Glossary","s":"CIA triad","t":"The three goals of information security: Confidentiality , Integrity and Availability ."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-classification","p":"Glossary","s":"Classification","t":"A label showing how sensitive information is (e.g. Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) and how it must be handled."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-compliance","p":"Glossary","s":"Compliance","t":"Meeting the requirements of laws, regulations, contracts, standards and internal policies, and being able to prove it."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-conflict-of-interest","p":"Glossary","s":"Conflict of interest","t":"A situation where personal interests could influence, or appear to influence, professional decisions. D"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-dpa","p":"Glossary","s":"Data Privacy Act of 2012 (RA 10173)","t":"The Philippine law protecting personal information, enforced by the National Privacy Commission."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-dpo","p":"Glossary","s":"Data Protection Officer (DPO)","t":"The person responsible for an organization's compliance with data privacy law."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-docs-as-code","p":"Glossary","s":"Docs as code","t":"Writing documentation in plain-text formats (such as Markdown), storing it in version control and reviewing changes like source code."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-document-control","p":"Glossary","s":"Document control","t":"The process that makes sure documents are identified, reviewed, approved, versioned and that readers always use the current version. E"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-escalation","p":"Glossary","s":"Escalation","t":"Passing an issue to a higher level of expertise or authority when it cannot be solved at the current level or within a set time. G"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-guideline","p":"Glossary","s":"Guideline","t":"A recommended practice. Unlike a policy, standard or procedure, a guideline is optional. I"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-incident","p":"Glossary","s":"Incident","t":"An unplanned interruption to a service, a reduction in its quality, or a security event that threatens systems or data."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-iso-27001","p":"Glossary","s":"ISO/IEC 27001","t":"The international standard for information security management systems (ISMS). Organizations can be certified against it."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-itil","p":"Glossary","s":"ITIL","t":"A widely used framework of best practices for IT service management, covering practices such as incident, change and knowledge management. K"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-kb","p":"Glossary","s":"Knowledge base (KB)","t":"A searchable collection of articles that answer common questions and problems. L"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-least-privilege","p":"Glossary","s":"Least privilege","t":"The principle that users and programs get only the minimum access they need to do their job. M"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-mfa","p":"Glossary","s":"Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)","t":"Signing in with two or more kinds of proof, such as a password plus a code from a phone app. N"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-npc","p":"Glossary","s":"National Privacy Commission (NPC)","t":"The Philippine government agency that administers and enforces the Data Privacy Act."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-nda","p":"Glossary","s":"Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA)","t":"A contract in which a person agrees not to disclose confidential information. P"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-breach","p":"Glossary","s":"Personal data breach","t":"A security incident that leads to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration or disclosure of, or access to, personal data."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-policy","p":"Glossary","s":"Policy","t":"A formal, management-approved statement of rules and their purpose. It says what must be done and why ."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-postmortem","p":"Glossary","s":"Post-incident review (postmortem)","t":"A meeting and report after an incident that examines what happened and how to prevent it, ideally in a blameless way."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-pam","p":"Glossary","s":"Privileged Access Management (PAM)","t":"Tools and processes that control, monitor and record the use of administrator accounts."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-procedure","p":"Glossary","s":"Procedure","t":"Step-by-step instructions for carrying out a task. It says how to do it. R"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-raci","p":"Glossary","s":"RACI matrix","t":"A table showing who is R esponsible, A ccountable, C onsulted and I nformed for each task."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-rpo","p":"Glossary","s":"Recovery Point Objective (RPO)","t":"The maximum amount of data, measured in time, that can be lost. An RPO of 24 hours requires at least daily backups."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-rto","p":"Glossary","s":"Recovery Time Objective (RTO)","t":"The maximum time allowed to restore a service after a failure."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-retention","p":"Glossary","s":"Retention policy","t":"Rules for how long each type of data or record is kept, and how it is disposed of."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-rca","p":"Glossary","s":"Root cause analysis (RCA)","t":"A method for finding the underlying cause of a problem, not just its symptoms. The 5 Whys is one RCA technique."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-runbook","p":"Glossary","s":"Runbook","t":"A document with the operational steps for running a service and responding to its alerts and failures. S"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-sla","p":"Glossary","s":"Service Level Agreement (SLA)","t":"An agreement that defines the expected level of service, such as availability or response time."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-standard","p":"Glossary","s":"Standard","t":"A mandatory, specific requirement that supports a policy, e.g. “passwords must be at least 12 characters”."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-sop","p":"Glossary","s":"Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)","t":"A documented, approved procedure for a routine task, followed the same way every time."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-style-guide","p":"Glossary","s":"Style guide","t":"A set of writing and formatting rules that keeps documents consistent. V"},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-version-control","p":"Glossary","s":"Version control","t":"Tracking changes to files over time so earlier versions can be compared or restored. Git is the most common tool."},{"u":"pages/glossary.html#term-vlan","p":"Glossary","s":"Virtual LAN (VLAN)","t":"A logically separate network on shared switches, used to isolate groups of devices such as servers, staff and guests."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"","t":"Topic 2 of 3 An IT policy is a formal, management-approved rule for how technology may be used and managed. Compliance means you can show that you follow your own policies and the laws, regulations and standards that apply to you. Documentation is how you show it."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#policy-hierarchy","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Policies, standards, procedures and guidelines","t":"These four words are often mixed up, but they form a clear hierarchy. Each level is more specific than the one above it, and each must agree with the level above. Figure 1. The policy hierarchy. Rules get more specific as you go down. Level Mandatory? Example: passwords Policy Yes “All accounts must be protected by strong authentication.” Standard Yes “Passwords must be at least 12 characters. MFA is required for administrator and remote access.” Procedure Yes “How to reset a user's password”, a step-by-step SOP . Guideline No “Consider a passphrase of four or more random words.”"},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#core-policies","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Core IT policies","t":"Most organizations keep a set of core IT policies. Open each one to see what it covers. Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) Purpose: Defines what users may and may not do with company computers, networks, email and internet access. Company systems are for business use; limited personal use may be allowed. No illegal content, piracy, harassment or attempts to bypass security controls. Users are told that activity may be monitored . This matters for privacy and ethics . Every user signs or electronically accepts the AUP before getting an account. Password & Authentication Policy Purpose: Protects accounts from unauthorized access. Minimum length (e.g. 12+ characters); long passphrases encouraged. MFA for administrators, remote access and email. Passwords are changed when compromise is suspected, rather than on a fixed schedule (current NIST SP 800-63B guidance). Never share passwords; store them only in an approved password manager. See the full policy example below. Access Control Policy Purpose: Makes sure people only have the access their job requires. Least privilege and need-to-know . Access is requested, approved and recorded. See the user provisioning SOP . Administrators use a separate admin account for privileged work. Access is reviewed regularly (e.g. quarterly) and removed on the day someone leaves. Backup & Recovery Policy Purpose: Makes sure data can be recovered after deletion, failure, ransomware or disaster. Follow the 3-2-1 rule : 3 copies, on 2 different media, 1 off-site. Define RPO (how much data you can afford to lose) and RTO (how fast you must recover). Test restores regularly and record the result. See the backup runbook . Patch & Vulnerability Management Policy Purpose: Keeps systems protected against known vulnerabilities. Patch timeframes by severity (e.g. critical within 7 days, others within 30 days). Test patches before production; schedule maintenance windows. Track exceptions for systems that cannot be patched. Change Management Policy Purpose: Prevents outages caused by unplanned or untested changes. Production changes need an approved change request with a test plan and a backout plan . Major changes are reviewed by a CAB . Emergency changes are documented afterwards. See the change request sample . Incident Response Policy Purpose: Defines how security and service incidents are reported, handled and learned from. Who to contact, severity levels and escalation paths. Phases: preparation → detection → containment → eradication → recovery → lessons learned. Legal duties such as personal data breach notification. See the incident report sample . Data Classification & Retention Policy Purpose: Defines how sensitive each type of data is, how long to keep it and how to dispose of it. Classes such as Public, Internal, Confidential and Restricted. Retention periods (e.g. logs for 1 year, financial records as required by law). Secure disposal: wipe or destroy media, for example following NIST SP 800-88. Remote Work & BYOD Policy Purpose: Sets rules for working outside the office and for personal devices (Bring Your Own Device). VPN required for internal systems; no public Wi-Fi without VPN. Devices must have screen lock, updates and disk encryption. The company can remotely wipe company data from a lost device."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#policy-structure","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Anatomy of a policy document","t":"Policies follow a standard structure so readers always know where to find things: Section What it answers Document control ID, version, owner, approver, effective date, next review Purpose Why does this policy exist? Scope Who and what does it apply to? Policy statements What are the rules? (numbered, using “must”/“must not”) Roles & responsibilities Who does what? Compliance & enforcement What happens if the policy is broken? Exceptions How to request one, and who can approve it Related documents & definitions Links to standards, procedures and the glossary Revision history What changed, when, and by whom"},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#policy-example","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Example: Password & Authentication Policy (excerpt)","t":"Document control Approved Document ID POL-SEC-002 Version 2.0 Owner Information Security Approved by Chief Information Officer Effective 2026-07-01 Next review 2027-07-01 1. Purpose. This policy protects Example Corp systems and data from unauthorized access by setting minimum requirements for authentication. 2. Scope. All employees, contractors and service accounts that access Example Corp systems. 3. Policy statements. 3.1 Passwords must be at least 12 characters long. 3.2 Passwords must not appear on lists of known breached passwords. The system checks this automatically. 3.3 MFA must be used for administrator accounts, VPN and email. 3.4 Passwords must be changed immediately if compromise is suspected. 3.5 Users must not share passwords or write them down in unsecured locations. 4. Enforcement. Violations may lead to disciplinary action under the Code of Conduct. Download a blank version from the policy template ."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#policy-lifecycle","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"The policy lifecycle","t":"Identify the need A new law, an audit finding, an incident or a new technology shows that a rule is needed. Draft The policy owner writes the policy using the standard template. Review IT, Information Security, HR and Legal review it for accuracy, fairness and legality. Approve Senior management signs it off. A policy without management approval has no authority. Communicate & train Publish it where staff can find it, train them, and collect acknowledgments. Enforce & monitor Apply it consistently, using technical controls where possible. Review Review at least once a year, or sooner if laws, systems or risks change."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#compliance","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"What is compliance?","t":"Compliance means meeting the requirements that apply to your organization, and being able to prove it . Requirements come from four places: Laws and regulations , e.g. the Data Privacy Act of 2012, which are mandatory. Contracts , e.g. PCI DSS, if you accept credit cards. Voluntary standards , e.g. ISO/IEC 27001 certification to win customer trust. Internal policies , the organization's own rules."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#laws-philippines","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Philippine laws every sysadmin should know","t":""},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#data-privacy-act","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Data Privacy Act of 2012 (Republic Act No. 10173)","t":"The main Philippine law protecting personal information. It is enforced by the National Privacy Commission (NPC) . Processing must follow the principles of transparency , legitimate purpose and proportionality . Organizations must implement organizational, physical and technical security measures . Sysadmins implement most of the technical ones: access control, encryption, logging, backups and secure disposal. Organizations must designate a Data Protection Officer (DPO) . Data subjects have rights, including the right to be informed, to access, to object, to correct and to erasure or blocking of their data. Under NPC rules, serious personal data breaches must be reported to the NPC and affected people within 72 hours of discovery. Why this matters to sysadmins The Act sets criminal penalties, including imprisonment and fines that can reach millions of pesos. They cover acts such as unauthorized processing, access due to negligence, and concealing a security breach . Sysadmins usually discover breaches first, so knowing the reporting procedure is part of the job."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#cybercrime-act","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Cybercrime Prevention Act of 2012 (Republic Act No. 10175)","t":"Defines and penalizes cybercrimes, including: Illegal access : accessing a computer system without right. Administrators must stay within their authorization. Illegal interception of non-public data transmissions. Data interference and system interference : altering, deleting or hindering data or systems without right. Misuse of devices : possessing or distributing tools or passwords meant for committing these offenses. It also requires service providers to preserve traffic data and subscriber information for a minimum period, which affects log retention policies."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#ecommerce-act","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Electronic Commerce Act of 2000 (Republic Act No. 8792)","t":"Gives electronic documents and signatures legal recognition. This is why properly controlled electronic records (logs, approvals, e-signed policies) can serve as evidence."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#international-frameworks","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"International standards and regulations","t":"Name Type Applies to Key point ISO/IEC 27001:2022 International standard (certifiable) Any organization Requires an Information Security Management System (ISMS). Annex A lists 93 controls. NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 Voluntary framework Any organization Six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover. CIS Critical Security Controls v8.1 Best-practice controls Any organization 18 prioritized controls, starting with hardware and software inventories. GDPR EU regulation Anyone processing personal data of people in the EU Fines up to €20 million or 4% of global annual turnover. PCI DSS v4.x Industry standard (contractual) Anyone handling payment card data 12 requirements for protecting cardholder data. HIPAA US law US healthcare organizations and their partners Protects patient health information. SOX US law US-listed public companies IT controls over financial reporting systems."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#documentation-evidence","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"How documentation proves compliance","t":"Auditors have a saying: “If it isn't documented, it didn't happen.” A policy only counts if you can produce evidence that it is followed. Requirement Evidence an auditor will ask for Users accept the AUP Signed or e-signed acknowledgment records Access is controlled Approved access requests and quarterly access review records Backups work Backup job logs and documented restore tests ( example ) Changes are controlled Approved change requests with test and backout plans ( example ) Incidents are handled Incident reports, breach assessments, notifications ( example ) Systems are patched Patch reports and vulnerability scan results Staff are trained Training attendance and assessment records"},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#audits","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Preparing for an IT audit","t":"An audit is an independent check that controls are designed well and actually work. Internal audits are done by the organization's own auditors; external audits by regulators, customers or certification bodies. Know the scope and criteria Which systems, which period, and against which standard or law? Gather evidence Collect current policies, procedures, logs and records in one place. Self-assess Run a gap analysis, for example with the checklist below. Fix the gaps Write a corrective action plan with owners and due dates. During the audit Answer honestly, show the evidence, and never guess or invent records. After the audit Address the findings, document corrective actions and update your policies."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#compliance-checklist","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Compliance readiness checklist","t":"Try it: tick the items as you check them. Your progress is saved in this browser. Example Corp: compliance self-assessment All IT policies are approved, dated and have a named owner Every user has accepted the Acceptable Use Policy A Data Protection Officer (DPO) is designated Personal data is inventoried: what data, where it is stored, who can access it User access is reviewed quarterly and leavers are removed on their last day MFA is enabled for administrators, VPN and email Backups run daily and a restore test is documented every month Every production change has an approved change request The incident response plan includes the 72-hour breach notification procedure Patches are applied within the timeframes in the patch policy Logs are retained and protected according to the retention policy All staff completed security and privacy awareness training this year"},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#consequences","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Consequences of non-compliance","t":"Legal: fines, lawsuits and, under the Data Privacy Act and Cybercrime Prevention Act, possible imprisonment. Financial: penalties, incident costs and lost business. Certification: loss of ISO certification or the right to process card payments. Reputation: customers and partners lose trust. Personal: disciplinary action against the individuals involved."},{"u":"pages/it-policies-compliance.html#summary","p":"IT Policies and Compliance","s":"Key takeaways","t":"Policy (what/why) → Standard (specific rules) → Procedure (how) → Guideline (advice). Every organization needs core policies: AUP, passwords, access, backup, patching, change, incident, data retention and remote work. Policies follow a lifecycle and must be approved, communicated, enforced and reviewed. In the Philippines, the Data Privacy Act (RA 10173) and Cybercrime Prevention Act (RA 10175) apply directly to sysadmin work. Compliance is proven with documentation and records . If it isn't documented, it didn't happen."},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"","t":"Change Request Request to apply the October 2026 Ubuntu security updates to web-01 and web-02 , one server at a time, so the company website stays online. Change details Awaiting CAB approval Change ID CHG-2026-0088 Change type Normal Priority Medium Risk Medium Requested by System Administrator Planned window 2026-11-07 22:00 – 24:00 (Sat) Affected service Company website Configuration items web-01, web-02, lb-01"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#change-types","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Change types (reference)","t":"Type Meaning Approval Standard Low-risk, routine, follows a documented procedure (e.g. creating a user) Pre-approved Normal Planned change that needs assessment (like this one) Change Advisory Board (CAB) Emergency Must be done immediately to restore service or fix a critical risk Emergency approver; documented afterwards"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#description","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Description and reason","t":"Apply all pending Ubuntu security updates, including kernel and OpenSSL packages, to both web servers. The October Ubuntu Security Notices include fixes rated High . The Patch Management Policy requires High-severity patches to be applied within 30 days . If we don't make this change: the servers stay exposed to known vulnerabilities and we fall out of compliance with the patch policy."},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#risk","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Risk and impact assessment","t":"The website runs on two servers behind a load balancer ( lb-01 ). Patching one server at a time means no downtime is expected , only reduced capacity for about 20 minutes per server. Risk Likelihood Impact Mitigation An update breaks the website (nginx fails to start) Low High Tested on staging first; VM snapshot before patching; backout plan below Server does not come back after reboot Low Medium Second server keeps the site online; console access confirmed Change runs past the window Low Low Two-hour window for about 45 minutes of work"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#pre-checks","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Pre-implementation checklist","t":"Complete before the window opens Updates tested on staging (web-stg-01) on 2026-11-04 with no errors Last night's backups verified for web-01 and web-02 Change approved by the CAB Stakeholders notified (see communication plan) Console access to both VMs confirmed Monitoring dashboard open during the change"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#implementation","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Implementation plan","t":"Do web-01 first. Repeat steps 1–7 for web-02 only after web-01 passes verification. Drain web-01 in the load balancer so it stops receiving new visitors. Wait until active connections reach 0. Take a VM snapshot named pre-CHG-2026-0088 . Refresh the package list and save the list of updates to this change record. sudo apt update apt list --upgradable 2>/dev/null | tee ~/CHG-2026-0088-web-01.txt Install the updates. sudo apt upgrade -y Reboot if required. [ -f /var/run/reboot-required ] && sudo reboot Verify the server (see verification below). Return web-01 to the load balancer pool and watch error rates for 10 minutes."},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#verification","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Verification","t":"systemctl is-active nginx # expected: active curl -sI http://localhost | head -n 1 # expected: HTTP/1.1 200 OK uname -r # expected: the new kernel version Then open https://www.example.com from an outside network and check the home page and login page."},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#backout","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Backout plan","t":"If verification fails and cannot be fixed within 15 minutes : Keep the server out of the load balancer pool. Revert the VM to the snapshot pre-CHG-2026-0088 (about 10 minutes). Verify the server with the same checks, then return it to the pool. Record what happened in this change record. Open an incident if users were affected. Note Delete the snapshot 7 days after a successful change. Old snapshots waste storage and slow down the VM."},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#communication","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Communication plan","t":"When Audience Message Channel 3 days before Web team, service desk Maintenance notice and window Email Window start IT on-call Change started IT chat channel Window end Web team, service desk Completed / backed out Email + ticket update"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#approvals","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Approvals","t":"Role Decision Date Comment Requester: System Administrator Submitted 2026-10-27 — Technical reviewer: Senior System Administrator Approved 2026-10-28 Added backout time limit Service owner: Web Team Lead Approved 2026-10-28 Saturday night window is fine CAB chair: IT Manager Pending — On the agenda for the 2026-11-03 CAB meeting"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#pir","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Post-implementation review","t":"To be completed after the change. Outcome — (Successful / Successful with issues / Backed out) Actual start / end — Issues encountered — Follow-up actions —"},{"u":"pages/samples/change-request.html#revision-history","p":"Monthly Security Patching of Web Servers","s":"Revision history","t":"Version Date Author Change 0.1 2026-10-27 System Administrator Initial request 0.2 2026-10-28 System Administrator Added 15-minute backout time limit (technical review) 0.3 2026-10-28 System Administrator Added communication plan; submitted to CAB"},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"","t":"Incident Report (Post-Incident Review) On 2026-10-06, staff could not open or save files on the shared drives for 85 minutes because a debug log filled the server's disk. Incident summary Resolved Incident ID INC-2026-0142 Severity SEV-2 (High) Date 2026-10-06 Duration 85 min (08:40–10:05 PHT) Service File shares (fs-01) Incident lead Senior System Administrator Report author System Administrator Approved by IT Manager, 2026-10-09 Blameless report This report focuses on systems and processes , not on blaming individuals. Its goal is to understand what happened and prevent it from happening again."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#summary","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Summary","t":"On 6 October 2026, from 08:40 to 10:05 Philippine Time, staff in four departments could not open or save files on the shared network drives hosted on fs-01 . The /var partition reached 100% capacity because a Samba debug log had grown to 38 GB. The log level had been raised to the maximum during troubleshooting four days earlier and was never set back. Service was restored by archiving the log, restoring the normal log level and restarting Samba. No data was lost."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#impact","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Impact","t":"Users affected: about 120 staff (Finance, HR, Sales and Operations). Business impact: 85 minutes without shared files; the payroll team's cut-off was moved by two hours. Data: no data lost or corrupted; files saved before 08:40 were intact. Security / privacy: no unauthorized access or disclosure (see privacy assessment )."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#timeline","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Timeline","t":"All times are Philippine Time (PHT, UTC+8). Date / time Event 10-02 15:10 While troubleshooting a printer-share issue, Samba's log level is raised to 10 (maximum detail). The temporary change is not recorded in a change request. 10-06 08:40 /var reaches 100%. Samba can no longer write its state files and starts refusing connections. 10-06 08:47 Service desk receives the first calls: “Z: drive not accessible”. Ticket opened. 10-06 08:55 On-call administrator acknowledges and declares a SEV-2 incident. 10-06 09:05 df -h shows /var at 100%. du identifies /var/log/samba/log.smbd at 38 GB. 10-06 09:20 Samba stopped; log copied to bkp-01 for analysis, then emptied. Log level set back to 1 . 10-06 09:35 Samba started. Access confirmed for test users. 10-06 10:05 All departments confirm access. Incident resolved. 10-06 10:30 Resolution notice emailed to all staff."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#detection","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Detection","t":"The incident was detected by users , seven minutes after it began. Monitoring did not alert because disk-space alerts were not configured on fs-01. This is a detection gap (see action 1)."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#root-cause","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Root cause analysis","t":"We used the 5 Whys technique, asking “why?” until we reached a cause we can fix: Why were the shares unavailable? Samba could not write to /var . Why could it not write? The /var partition was 100% full. Why was it full? The Samba log grew to 38 GB in four days. Why did the log grow so fast? Debug logging (level 10) was left on after troubleshooting. Why was it left on? The temporary change was not documented, so no one had a task to revert it. Log rotation had no size limit, and there was no disk-space alert. Root cause An undocumented temporary configuration change , combined with no size limit on log rotation and no disk-usage monitoring ."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#resolution","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Resolution","t":"Commands run during recovery, recorded for future reference: df -h /var # confirmed 100% used sudo du -xh /var --max-depth=3 | sort -h | tail -n 5 # found log.smbd (38G) sudo systemctl stop smbd sudo rsync -a /var/log/samba/log.smbd backup@bkp-01:/incidents/INC-2026-0142/ sudo truncate -s 0 /var/log/samba/log.smbd sudo sed -i 's/log level = 10/log level = 1/' /etc/samba/smb.conf testparm -s 2>/dev/null | grep \"log level\" # verify the new setting sudo systemctl start smbd"},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#privacy-assessment","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Data privacy assessment","t":"The incident affected availability only . No personal data was accessed by unauthorized people, disclosed, altered or lost. It is therefore not a reportable personal data breach under the Data Privacy Act of 2012. The Data Protection Officer reviewed this assessment and agreed on 2026-10-07. Why record this? Even when no notification is needed, writing down the assessment and who approved it shows regulators and auditors that the question was considered. See Data Privacy Act ."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#actions","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Corrective and preventive actions","t":"# Action Owner Due Status 1 Add disk-usage alerts at 80% (warning) and 90% (critical) on all servers System Administrator 2026-10-13 Done 2 Limit Samba log size with max log size and logrotate (100 MB per file) System Administrator 2026-10-10 Done 3 Update the change policy: temporary changes, including debug settings, need a change record with a revert date IT Manager 2026-10-20 Done 4 Move /var/log to its own partition on fs-01 Senior System Administrator 2026-11-15 In progress 5 Add a “disk full” section to the file server runbook System Administrator 2026-10-31 Open"},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#lessons","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Lessons learned","t":"What went well Clear escalation: the incident was declared within 15 minutes. The cause was found quickly with standard commands. Users received a clear resolution notice. What we will improve Temporary changes must be documented, even when they seem small. Monitoring must alert us before users notice. Status updates should go out during the incident, not only after."},{"u":"pages/samples/incident-report.html#revision-history","p":"File Server Outage: Disk Full on fs-01","s":"Revision history","t":"Version Date Author Change 0.1 2026-10-06 System Administrator Initial timeline and summary 0.2 2026-10-08 System Administrator Added root cause analysis and actions after the review meeting 1.0 2026-10-09 IT Manager Approved"},{"u":"pages/samples/index.html","p":"Sample Documents","s":"","t":"Demonstration These are the documents a real system administration team writes and maintains. They apply everything from the three report topics, written for Example Corp , a fictional company. About the examples Example Corp, its people, servers and IP addresses are fictional. The domain example.com and the public address range 203.0.113.0/24 are reserved for documentation (RFC 2606 and RFC 5737), as recommended in our style rules ."},{"u":"pages/samples/index.html#documents","p":"Sample Documents","s":"The documents","t":"Approved SOP-ACC-001 New User Account Provisioning. A standard operating procedure with Linux and Windows steps. Approved RB-BKP-002 Nightly Backup & Restore. An operational runbook with checks, restore steps and troubleshooting. Approved INC-2026-0142 File Server Outage. A blameless incident report with timeline, root cause and corrective actions. In Review CHG-2026-0088 Web Server Patching. A change request with risk assessment, backout plan and approvals. Approved NET-001 Network & Asset Inventory. A network diagram, IP plan, server inventory and license register."},{"u":"pages/samples/index.html#what-to-notice","p":"Sample Documents","s":"What to notice in each document","t":"Practice Where to find it Document control block (ID, version, owner, status, review date) Top of every sample Consistent structure: purpose, scope, steps, verification, rollback SOP Commands with expected output, plus a copy button SOP procedure , runbook daily check Troubleshooting tables and escalation paths Runbook Timeline, 5 Whys and corrective actions with owners Incident report Risk assessment, backout plan and an approval trail Change request Diagrams and tables as reference documentation Network & inventory Revision history at the end of every document Bottom of every sample Cross-references between related documents “Related documents” sections"},{"u":"pages/samples/index.html#how-they-connect","p":"Sample Documents","s":"How the documents connect","t":"Documents rarely stand alone. In this example set: The incident report found a gap, an undocumented temporary change, which led to updates in the change management policy and the runbooks . The change request uses the server inventory to list affected systems and relies on backups from the runbook as a safety net. The SOP implements the Password and Access Control policies. Want to write your own? Start from the templates ."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"","t":"Reference Documentation The map of Example Corp's IT environment: how the network is built, which addresses are used, which servers exist, and which software licenses we own. Document control Approved Document ID NET-001 Version 3.4 Owner Network & Systems Team Last physical audit 2026-10-20 Next review 2027-01-20 (quarterly) Classification Confidential Why “Confidential”? A network map is as useful to an attacker as it is to an administrator. Share this document only with staff who need it, and never post it publicly."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#network-diagram","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"Network diagram","t":"Figure 1. Logical network diagram (NET-001, v3.4). The master copy is kept in network/NET-001_Network-Diagram.drawio ."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#ip-plan","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"VLAN and IP address plan","t":"VLAN Name Subnet Gateway Addressing Access rules 10 Servers 192.168.10.0/24 .1 Static Staff may reach only published services (file, web, DNS) 20 Staff 192.168.20.0/24 .1 DHCP .100–.250 Internet + server services 30 Guest Wi-Fi 192.168.30.0/24 .1 DHCP .50–.250 Internet only; blocked from all internal VLANs 99 Management 192.168.99.0/24 .1 Static Reachable only from IT admin workstations"},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#server-inventory","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"Server inventory","t":"Hostname Role OS / platform IP address Owner Support until fw-01 Firewall, VPN gateway Firewall appliance 192.168.99.1 Network 2028-03 sw-core-01 Core switch Managed L3 switch 192.168.99.2 Network 2029-06 dc-01 Domain controller, DNS, DHCP Windows Server 2025 192.168.10.10 Systems 2029-11 fs-01 File server (Samba) Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS 192.168.10.20 Systems 2029-04 bkp-01 Backup server (NFS) Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS 192.168.10.30 Systems 2029-04 lb-01 Load balancer (HAProxy) Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS 192.168.10.40 Systems 2029-04 web-01 Web server (nginx) Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS 192.168.10.41 Web team 2029-04 web-02 Web server (nginx) Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS 192.168.10.42 Web team 2029-04 srv-01 Linux application server Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS 192.168.10.50 Systems 2029-04 “Support until” is the earlier of the hardware warranty and the OS standard support end date."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#software-licenses","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"Software license register","t":"Software License type Owned In use Renewal Microsoft 365 Business Standard Subscription, per user 130 121 2027-06-30 Windows Server 2025 Standard Per core + client access licenses (CALs) 16 cores / 130 CALs 16 / 121 Perpetual Endpoint protection (EDR) Subscription, per device 140 128 2027-01-31 Ubuntu Server Open source (free) — 6 servers N/A Tip Compare “Owned” and “In use” every quarter. Using more than you own breaks license terms (see Ethics: licensing ). Paying for far more than you use wastes money."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#naming","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"Naming conventions","t":"Hostnames: role-NN , lowercase, two-digit number, e.g. web-01 . Network devices: type-location-NN , e.g. sw-core-01 . Gateways: always .1 in each subnet. Static server addresses: grouped by role in tens ( .10 directory, .20 files, .30 backup, .40 web)."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#credentials","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"Credentials","t":"Credentials are not stored here Administrator passwords and keys for every device above are stored in the password manager collection Infrastructure . Access is limited to the Network & Systems Team and is logged."},{"u":"pages/samples/network-inventory.html#revision-history","p":"Network & Asset Inventory","s":"Revision history","t":"Version Date Author Change 3.2 2026-04-18 Network Engineer Added VLAN 30 (Guest Wi-Fi) 3.3 2026-07-14 System Administrator Added lb-01 and web-02 (load-balanced website) 3.4 2026-10-20 System Administrator Quarterly audit: updated license counts and support dates; dc-01 upgraded to Windows Server 2025"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"","t":"Runbook Operational guide for the on-call administrator: how the fs-01 backup works, how to check it, how to restore data, and what to do when it fails. Document control Approved Document ID RB-BKP-002 Version 2.1 Owner IT Operations Service File server (fs-01) Last restore test 2026-10-15 Next review 2027-04-15 Classification Internal"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#overview","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Service overview","t":"Protected server fs-01 (192.168.10.20), Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS, Samba file shares Data backed up /srv/data : department shared folders (about 180 GB) Backup target bkp-01 (192.168.10.30), mounted on fs-01 at /mnt/backups Off-site copy Encrypted cloud object storage, copied nightly at 03:00 Schedule Every day at 01:30 Retention 14 days on bkp-01 · 90 days off-site RPO / RTO 24 hours / 4 hours"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#how-it-works","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"How the backup works","t":"Figure 1. Backup data flow for fs-01. The backup is a shell script run by cron. It creates a dated, compressed archive, records a SHA-256 checksum so corruption can be detected, and deletes archives older than 14 days. #!/usr/bin/env bash # Nightly backup of /srv/data on fs-01. Documented in RB-BKP-002. set -euo pipefail STAMP=\"$(date +%F)\" # e.g. 2026-10-28 DEST=\"/mnt/backups/fs-01\" # NFS share on bkp-01 ARCHIVE=\"$DEST/fs-01-data-$STAMP.tar.gz\" LOG=\"/var/log/backup/nightly-$STAMP.log\" { echo \"[$(date '+%F %T')] Backup started\" tar -czf \"$ARCHIVE\" -C /srv data sha256sum \"$ARCHIVE\" > \"$ARCHIVE.sha256\" find \"$DEST\" -name 'fs-01-data-*' -mtime +14 -delete echo \"[$(date '+%F %T')] Backup finished ($(du -h \"$ARCHIVE\" | cut -f1))\" } >> \"$LOG\" 2>&1 # min hour day month weekday user command 30 1 * * * root /usr/local/sbin/nightly-backup.sh"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#daily-check","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Daily check (every morning)","t":"Confirm last night's backup finished. tail -n 2 /var/log/backup/nightly-$(date +%F).log Expected: a line ending in Backup finished (…G) . Check that the archive exists and its size looks normal (about 60–70 GB compressed). ls -lh /mnt/backups/fs-01/ | tail -n 4 Verify the checksum. sha256sum -c /mnt/backups/fs-01/fs-01-data-$(date +%F).tar.gz.sha256 Expected: /mnt/backups/fs-01/fs-01-data-2026-10-28.tar.gz: OK Record the result in the daily operations log (ticket queue “Backups”)."},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#restore-file","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Restore a single file or folder","t":"Before you start Confirm the request comes from someone who is allowed to access that data. Restoring a file for someone without access would be a privacy violation (see Ethics: privacy ). Find the archive from before the file was lost. ls /mnt/backups/fs-01/ Confirm the file is in the archive. tar -tzf /mnt/backups/fs-01/fs-01-data-2026-10-27.tar.gz | grep 'finance/budget-2026.xlsx' Extract it to a temporary folder. mkdir -p /tmp/restore tar -xzf /mnt/backups/fs-01/fs-01-data-2026-10-27.tar.gz -C /tmp/restore data/finance/budget-2026.xlsx Copy it back under a new name so nothing newer is overwritten. sudo cp -p /tmp/restore/data/finance/budget-2026.xlsx \\ /srv/data/finance/budget-2026.restored-2026-10-27.xlsx rm -rf /tmp/restore Tell the user where the restored file is, and close the ticket. Warning Never extract an archive directly over /srv/data . It overwrites every newer file in the share."},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#full-restore","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Full restore (disaster recovery)","t":"Use this only when fs-01 or its data is lost. Target: service restored within the 4-hour RTO . Declare an incident and notify the IT Manager. Open an incident record. Build or repair the server using the fs-01 build document, with the same hostname and IP. Mount the backup share and verify the latest archive. sudo mount -a sha256sum -c /mnt/backups/fs-01/fs-01-data-2026-10-28.tar.gz.sha256 If bkp-01 is also lost, retrieve the archive from off-site storage (RB-BKP-003). Restore the data. sudo tar -xzf /mnt/backups/fs-01/fs-01-data-2026-10-28.tar.gz -C /srv Start the file sharing service and test from a client PC. sudo systemctl restart smbd systemctl is-active smbd Notify users that service is back and that changes made after the backup time may need to be redone. Then complete the incident report."},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#restore-test","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Monthly restore test","t":"Why we test A backup that has never been restored is only a hope. Each month, restore sample files to a test folder and record the result here. Auditors will ask for this evidence. Date Archive What was restored Time Result 2026-10-15 …2026-10-14.tar.gz 20 random files, 3 folders 6 min Pass 2026-09-15 …2026-09-14.tar.gz Full restore to test VM 2 h 10 min Pass 2026-08-14 …2026-08-13.tar.gz 20 random files — Fail checksum mismatch, see INC-2026-0117"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#troubleshooting","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Troubleshooting","t":"Symptom Likely cause Action No log file for today Cron did not run, or fs-01 was down at 01:30 Check systemctl status cron and grep CRON /var/log/syslog . Run the script manually with sudo . No space left on device in the log The backup volume on bkp-01 is full Check df -h /mnt/backups . Confirm old archives were deleted. Escalate to L2. computed checksum did NOT match The archive is corrupted Do not rely on that archive. Re-run the backup and open an incident. Stale file handle or /mnt/backups is empty The NFS mount to bkp-01 dropped Check with findmnt /mnt/backups , remount with sudo mount -a , and check that bkp-01 is up."},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#escalation","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Escalation","t":"Level Who When Contact L1 On-call system administrator Any backup alert On-call roster in the ITSM tool L2 Senior system administrator Not fixed within 1 hour, or two failed backups in a row ext. 1204 L3 IT Manager Data loss, or restore will exceed the 4-hour RTO ext. 1200 DPO Data Protection Officer Personal data may be lost or exposed dpo@corp.example.com"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#related","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Related documents","t":"Backup & Recovery Policy ( summary ) RB-BKP-003 Off-site Backup Retrieval NET-001 Network & Asset Inventory ( sample ) INC-2026-0142 File Server Outage ( sample )"},{"u":"pages/samples/runbook-backup-restore.html#revision-history","p":"Nightly File Server Backup & Restore","s":"Revision history","t":"Version Date Author Change 1.0 2025-06-02 System Administrator First approved version 2.0 2026-03-10 Senior System Administrator Added off-site copy and SHA-256 checksums; retention changed to 14 days 2.1 2026-10-15 System Administrator Added NFS troubleshooting row and the October restore test result"},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"","t":"Standard Operating Procedure How to create a user account for a new employee or contractor on Example Corp's Linux servers and Windows Active Directory domain. Document control Approved Document ID SOP-ACC-001 Version 1.2 Owner IT Operations Author System Administrator Reviewed by Senior System Administrator Approved by IT Manager Effective date 2026-09-01 Next review 2027-09-01 Classification Internal"},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#purpose","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"1. Purpose","t":"This procedure makes sure every new account is approved , created consistently , given only the access the person needs , and recorded . It implements the Password & Authentication Policy (POL-SEC-002) and the Access Control Policy (POL-SEC-003)."},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#scope","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"2. Scope","t":"In scope: standard user accounts for employees and contractors on Linux servers (Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS) and the Windows Active Directory domain corp.example.com . Out of scope: administrator accounts and service accounts. These need separate approval from the IT Manager (SOP-ACC-004)."},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#roles","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"3. Roles and responsibilities","t":"Role Responsibility HR Confirms employment and start date; opens the new-hire request. Department head Approves which systems and groups the person needs. System administrator Creates the account by following this SOP and records the work in the ticket. Service desk Gives the user their temporary password and helps with first login."},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#prerequisites","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"4. Prerequisites","t":"Do not start until all of these are true: There is an approved access request ticket (e.g. REQ-2026-0311 ) listing the required groups. HR has confirmed the employment and start date. The user has accepted the Acceptable Use Policy . You are logged in with your own admin account , not a shared or root account. You know the username, which follows the naming convention: first initial + surname , in lowercase (e.g. Juan Dela Cruz → jdelacruz ). Danger Never add a user to an administrator group ( sudo , Domain Admins ) under this SOP. Administrator access needs separate written approval from the IT Manager."},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#procedure","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"5. Procedure","t":"Choose the platform. The tabs remember your choice, and the verification section below switches with them. Connect to the server with your personal admin account. ssh your-admin-user@srv-01.corp.example.com Check that the username is not already taken. id jdelacruz Expected output: id: 'jdelacruz': no such user Create the account with a home directory and the Bash shell. sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c \"Juan Dela Cruz\" jdelacruz Set a temporary password and force a change at first login. sudo passwd jdelacruz # enter a temporary password (12+ characters) sudo chage -d 0 jdelacruz # user must change it at first login Add the user to the groups approved in the ticket. sudo usermod -aG developers jdelacruz Warning Always use -aG (append). Using -G alone removes the user from every group not listed. Open PowerShell with your admin account on a management workstation and load the AD module. Import-Module ActiveDirectory Check that the username is not already taken. Get-ADUser -Filter \"SamAccountName -eq 'jdelacruz'\" Expected: no output. Create the account in the Staff organizational unit. New-ADUser -Name \"Juan Dela Cruz\" -GivenName \"Juan\" -Surname \"Dela Cruz\" ` -SamAccountName \"jdelacruz\" -UserPrincipalName \"jdelacruz@corp.example.com\" ` -Path \"OU=Staff,DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com\" ` -AccountPassword (Read-Host -AsSecureString \"Temporary password\") ` -ChangePasswordAtLogon $true -Enabled $true Add the user to the groups approved in the ticket. Add-ADGroupMember -Identity \"Developers\" -Members \"jdelacruz\""},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#handover","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"Hand over the account (both platforms)","t":"Give the temporary password through a separate channel. Tell the user in person, or have the service desk send it by SMS. Help the user enrol in MFA at first login. Required by POL-SEC-002 for email and VPN. Record the work in the ticket. Note the username, groups added, date and your name, then close the ticket. Warning Never send the username and temporary password together in the same email or chat message."},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#verification","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"6. Verification","t":"id jdelacruz sudo chage -l jdelacruz | head -n 1 Expected output (the IDs may differ): uid=1005(jdelacruz) gid=1005(jdelacruz) groups=1005(jdelacruz),1002(developers) Last password change : password must be changed Get-ADUser jdelacruz -Properties MemberOf, PasswordLastSet | Select-Object Name, Enabled, PasswordLastSet, MemberOf Expected: Enabled is True , PasswordLastSet is empty (the password must be changed at logon), and MemberOf lists only the approved groups. Completion checklist Access request ticket is approved Username follows the naming convention and was not already in use Account created; password change forced at first login Only the approved groups were added (no admin groups) Temporary password given through a separate channel Ticket updated with details and closed"},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#troubleshooting","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"7. Troubleshooting","t":"Problem Likely cause Solution useradd: user 'jdelacruz' already exists The username is taken, possibly by a former employee. Use the next variation (e.g. jdelacruz2 ). Never reuse a former employee's account. usermod: group 'developers' does not exist Typo, or the group has not been created. List groups with getent group and confirm the name in the ticket. “The password does not meet the length, complexity, or history requirement” The temporary password breaks the domain password policy. Use a temporary password of 12 or more characters. “Access is denied” in PowerShell PowerShell is not running under your admin account. Start PowerShell with Run as different user and use your admin account. User cannot log in over SSH The user's group is not allowed in the SSH configuration. Check AllowGroups in /etc/ssh/sshd_config ; ask for approval before changing it."},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#rollback","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"8. Rollback","t":"If an account was created by mistake, disable it, don't delete it . That keeps an audit trail. Then follow the deprovisioning SOP (SOP-ACC-002). sudo usermod --lock --expiredate 1 jdelacruz # lock the password and expire the account Disable-ADAccount -Identity jdelacruz"},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#related","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"9. Related documents","t":"POL-SEC-002 Password & Authentication Policy ( excerpt ) POL-SEC-003 Access Control Policy ( summary ) SOP-ACC-002 Account Deprovisioning SOP-ACC-004 Administrator and Service Accounts Glossary"},{"u":"pages/samples/sop-user-provisioning.html#revision-history","p":"New User Account Provisioning","s":"10. Revision history","t":"Version Date Author Change 0.1 2025-08-04 System Administrator Initial draft (Linux only) 1.0 2025-09-01 System Administrator Approved by IT Manager 1.1 2026-02-16 System Administrator Added Windows Active Directory steps 1.2 2026-09-01 Senior System Administrator Added rule to deliver temporary passwords through a separate channel; added MFA enrolment step"},{"u":"pages/templates.html","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"","t":"Resources Templates are the easiest way to enforce documentation standards. Everyone starts from the same structure, so nothing important gets forgotten and every document looks familiar."},{"u":"pages/templates.html#how-to-use","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"How to use these templates","t":"Copy or download the template you need. The templates are in Markdown, which works in Git, most wikis and many ticketing tools. Replace every [placeholder] with real content. Remove sections that truly don't apply, but think twice first. Start at version 0.1 with status “Draft”. The document becomes 1.0 only after review and approval. Store it in the agreed location using the naming convention . SOP Routine step-by-step tasks Incident report What went wrong and how to prevent it Change request Planned changes to production KB article Short fixes for common problems IT policy Formal rules approved by management"},{"u":"pages/templates.html#tpl-sop","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)","t":"For routine tasks that must be done the same way every time. See the completed example . Download .md # [SOP-AREA-NNN] [Procedure title] | Field | Value | |----------------|-------------------------------------| | Document ID | SOP-[AREA]-[NNN] | | Version | 0.1 | | Status | Draft | | Owner | [Team] | | Author | [Name / role] | | Approved by | [Name / role] | | Effective date | [YYYY-MM-DD] | | Next review | [YYYY-MM-DD] | | Classification | [Public / Internal / Confidential] | ## 1. Purpose [Why this procedure exists, in one or two sentences.] ## 2. Scope - In scope: [systems, people, situations] - Out of scope: [what this SOP does not cover] ## 3. Roles and responsibilities | Role | Responsibility | |--------|----------------| | [Role] | [What they do] | ## 4. Prerequisites - [ ] [Approval or ticket number] - [ ] [Access and tools needed] ## 5. Procedure 1. [Start with a verb. One action per step.] ```bash [command] ``` Expected result: [what the reader should see] 2. [Next action.] ## 6. Verification [How to confirm the procedure worked.] ## 7. Troubleshooting | Problem | Likely cause | Solution | |---------|--------------|----------| | | | | ## 8. Rollback [How to undo the work safely.] ## 9. Related documents - [Document ID and title] ## 10. Revision history | Version | Date | Author | Change | |---------|------------|--------|---------------| | 0.1 | YYYY-MM-DD | [Name] | Initial draft |"},{"u":"pages/templates.html#tpl-incident","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"Incident report","t":"Written after a service disruption or security incident. Keep it blameless. See the completed example . Download .md # [INC-YYYY-NNNN] [Short incident title] | Field | Value | |---------------|---------------------------------------------| | Incident ID | INC-[YYYY]-[NNNN] | | Severity | [SEV-1 / SEV-2 / SEV-3] | | Status | [Open / Resolved / Closed] | | Start – end | [YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM] – [HH:MM] ([time zone]) | | Service(s) | [Affected services] | | Incident lead | [Name / role] | | Report author | [Name / role] | > Blameless report: focus on systems and processes, not people. ## Summary [What happened, the impact, the cause and the fix, in 3–5 sentences.] ## Impact - Users affected: - Business impact: - Data affected: ## Timeline | Time | Event | |------|-------| | | | ## Detection [How was it detected? How could we detect it sooner?] ## Root cause analysis (5 Whys) 1. Why ...? 2. Why ...? 3. Why ...? 4. Why ...? 5. Why ...? **Root cause:** [ ] ## Resolution [What was done to restore service, including commands.] ## Data privacy assessment [Was personal data accessed, disclosed, altered or lost? Is notification to the NPC required within 72 hours? Reviewed by the DPO on YYYY-MM-DD.] ## Corrective and preventive actions | # | Action | Owner | Due date | Status | |---|--------|-------|----------|--------| | 1 | | | | Open | ## Lessons learned - What went well: - What we will improve:"},{"u":"pages/templates.html#tpl-change","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"Change request","t":"Required before any planned change to production systems. See the completed example . Download .md # [CHG-YYYY-NNNN] [Change title] | Field | Value | |---------------------|------------------------------------| | Change ID | CHG-[YYYY]-[NNNN] | | Change type | [Standard / Normal / Emergency] | | Priority | [Low / Medium / High] | | Risk | [Low / Medium / High] | | Requested by | [Name / role] | | Planned window | [YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM – HH:MM] | | Affected service | [Service] | | Configuration items | [Hostnames] | ## Description and reason [What will change and why. What happens if we don't do it?] ## Risk and impact assessment | Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation | |------|------------|--------|------------| | | | | | ## Pre-implementation checklist - [ ] Tested in a non-production environment - [ ] Backups verified - [ ] Stakeholders notified - [ ] Change approved ## Implementation plan 1. [Step] 2. [Step] ## Verification [How to confirm the change was successful.] ## Backout plan [Exact steps to undo the change, and the time limit before backing out.] ## Communication plan | When | Audience | Message | Channel | |------|----------|---------|---------| | | | | | ## Approvals | Role | Name | Decision | Date | |------|------|----------|------| | | | | | ## Post-implementation review - Outcome: - Actual start / end: - Issues encountered:"},{"u":"pages/templates.html#tpl-kb","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"Knowledge base (KB) article","t":"A short, searchable answer to a common problem, written for users or the service desk. Download .md # [KB-NNNN] How to [solve a specific problem] **Applies to:** [Systems / versions] **Audience:** [End users / Service desk / Administrators] **Last updated:** [YYYY-MM-DD] · **Owner:** [Team] ## Problem [What the user sees, including the exact error message.] ## Cause [Why it happens, in plain language.] ## Solution 1. [Step] 2. [Step] ## Still not working? Contact the service desk at [extension / email] and mention **KB-NNNN**. ## Related articles - [KB-NNNN Title]"},{"u":"pages/templates.html#tpl-policy","p":"Documentation Templates","s":"IT policy","t":"A formal rule approved by management. See the policy example . Download .md # [POL-AREA-NNN] [Policy name] | Field | Value | |----------------|---------------------------| | Document ID | POL-[AREA]-[NNN] | | Version | 0.1 | | Status | Draft | | Owner | [Department] | | Approved by | [Senior management role] | | Effective date | [YYYY-MM-DD] | | Next review | [YYYY-MM-DD] | ## 1. Purpose [Why this policy exists.] ## 2. Scope [Who and what this policy applies to.] ## 3. Policy statements - 3.1 [Subject] **must** [requirement]. - 3.2 [Subject] **must not** [prohibited action]. ## 4. Roles and responsibilities | Role | Responsibility | |------|----------------| | | | ## 5. Compliance and enforcement [How compliance is checked and what happens if the policy is violated.] ## 6. Exceptions [How to request an exception and who approves it.] ## 7. Related documents and definitions - [Standards, procedures, glossary terms] ## 8. Revision history | Version | Date | Author | Change | |---------|------|--------|--------| | 0.1 | | | |"}];
